*The basic structure of the specification*

The specification is made of three main parts: its guidelines, its base and its technical appendix. Each part of the specification answers a question of its own:

- the guidelines say how the specification is kept, changed and implemented;
- the base specification says what the system does;
- the technical appendix says how the system is built.

*Reason.* A reader who knows which question a part answers knows where to look for a rule and where to write one. The answers change at different rates. How the specification is kept hardly changes. What the system does changes with its business logic or its core functionality. How the system is built changes most often. Held apart, each part changes at its own rate.

