**The structure of the guidelines**

2.1 · `s-a4rbry4hsu34hy8`

The guidelines consist of a description of the rules for keeping the specification and of the requirements for the tools that ensure those rules are carried out.

The rules state what is held, what is checked, when, and what a failure costs.

The requirements for the tools stand in the appendix of the tools. It describes how one toolset carries those rules out:

- its commands;
- its hooks;
- where it keeps the files;
- how it reads code;
- who runs its reviewer.

The appendix of the tools is not the technical appendix. The technical appendix describes how the system is built, never the tools of the specification.

*Why:* The tools can change while the rule they carry out stays, so the requirements for them stand apart from the rules.

The tools hold the specification and the code to each other rather than make up the system. That is why they are described apart from the technical appendix.

